[THOUGHT] The chest X-ray findings are highly concerning for a traumatic aortic injury (TAI), specifically aortic rupture or pseudoaneurysm. Key red flags:  
- Widened mediastinum (>8 cm on PA view, or >6 cm on supine AP) — the most sensitive radiographic sign of TAI;  
- Depression of the left mainstem bronchus — caused by aortic arch hematoma compressing the bronchus;  
- Deviation of the nasogastric tube to the right — suggests mediastinal shift or mass effect from hematoma;  
- Rib fracture is consistent with high-force deceleration but is nonspecific.  
While pneumothorax or hemothorax could explain decreased breath sounds, they do *not* cause widened mediastinum, bronchial depression, or NG tube deviation. The combination of acute respiratory distress, drowsiness (suggesting hypotension/shock), and these CXR findings strongly points to catastrophic thoracic aortic injury — a leading cause of early death in motor vehicle crashes. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate confirmation and intervention.

[TEST] Contrast-enhanced CT angiography (CTA) of the chest.

